WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:02.210
Ready?

00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:08.091
When you fly over Antarctica

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:10.468
all you see is white.

00:00:12.303 --> 00:00:14.014
You don't hear any birds.

00:00:14.014 --> 00:00:16.182
You don't see any plants.

00:00:16.266 --> 00:00:21.730
It looks empty, but there's something hiding

00:00:21.771 --> 00:00:23.690
down below this ice.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.401
In 1970, a group of Soviet scientists

00:00:26.401 --> 00:00:29.946
started drilling right here. For 28 years

00:00:29.988 --> 00:00:32.449
they kept going and going

00:00:32.574 --> 00:00:36.077
until after nearly 4000m.

00:00:36.161 --> 00:00:38.538
They suddenly stopped

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:41.875
because they had found something unimaginable.

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:43.626
Liquid water.

00:00:43.626 --> 00:00:45.253
A secret lake,

00:00:45.253 --> 00:00:49.924
down below all this ice that had been completely sealed off

00:00:49.924 --> 00:00:54.137
from the rest of the world for 15 million years.

00:00:54.137 --> 00:00:55.305
How?!

00:00:55.305 --> 00:00:59.768
And... is there something alive in there?

00:00:59.768 --> 00:01:02.020
Creatures that have been evolving separately

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:06.066
from the rest of the planet for 15 million years?

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:09.652
So scientists started searching
for more of these secret lakes.

00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:13.073
And as they did, they realized there is

00:01:13.198 --> 00:01:18.203
way more going on under Antarctica than we ever thought.

00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:20.955
I needed to know

00:01:20.955 --> 00:01:24.042
the truth about Earth's most remote continent.

00:01:24.042 --> 00:01:28.463
So I've come all the way to Antarctica to show you

00:01:28.505 --> 00:01:32.050
what's really happening down below this ice,

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.385
and exactly what they discovered

00:01:34.385 --> 00:01:37.806
when they finally broke inside.

00:01:38.056 --> 00:01:40.058
Bring out the drill!

00:01:47.053 --> 00:01:48.930
Ready?

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:51.694
If you wanted to drill to the bottom of Antarctica.

00:01:51.736 --> 00:01:53.780
The first thing you need to realize is

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.658
this ice is way thicker than you think.

00:01:56.699 --> 00:01:58.785
If we were to take a chunk out of this ice

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:01.538
and place it on the surface, at its thickest,

00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:04.290
it would be this tall.

00:02:05.875 --> 00:02:07.627
Wow.

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:10.171
That's nearly the height of Mount Kilimanjaro.

00:02:10.213 --> 00:02:13.466
You would have to stack the tallest building in the world

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:16.386
six times to reach the top of that!

00:02:16.511 --> 00:02:18.972
And this isn't just one uniform block.

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:21.599
It's full of all different kinds of ice.

00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:24.811
And if you looked into the hole left behind

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:27.689
by our chunk, you'd see

00:02:27.689 --> 00:02:28.481
land!

00:02:28.648 --> 00:02:30.817
So as we start drilling, at first

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:34.154
we're passing through snow,
which is surprisingly scarce here

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:37.240
because Antarctica is the driest, coldest,

00:02:37.282 --> 00:02:39.701
windiest continent on Earth.

00:02:39.742 --> 00:02:41.744
It's technically a desert.

00:02:41.786 --> 00:02:46.082
I can confirm it is very cold and very windy.

00:02:46.124 --> 00:02:48.376
On average, it only snows a couple centimeters

00:02:48.376 --> 00:02:51.337
a year here, meaning the snow just a few feet under
your boots

00:02:51.379 --> 00:02:55.091
could be from a decade ago,
but as our drill makes its way through this snow,

00:02:55.133 --> 00:02:57.385
the texture starts to change.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.972
"The snow then, of course, keeps accumulating
and the deeper layers

00:03:01.222 --> 00:03:03.474
become consolidated into firn."

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:05.685
That's Dr. Steven Chown, a biologist

00:03:05.727 --> 00:03:08.188
who specializes in life on Antarctica.

00:03:08.229 --> 00:03:11.691
He's leading the trip I'm on with the White Desert
Foundation's “Science Week.”

00:03:11.733 --> 00:03:14.110
It supports scientific research in Antarctica.

00:03:14.152 --> 00:03:18.406
The “firn” that he's talking about
is the stage between snow and ice.

00:03:18.406 --> 00:03:20.575
It looks like a frozen sponge.

00:03:20.575 --> 00:03:22.410
So we're drilling through this frozen sponge.

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:25.705
And before we reach the point
where it actually turns into ice,

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:28.041
we hit something alarming.

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:34.255
I don't know why

00:03:34.255 --> 00:03:36.966
driving over it feels so much scarier than walking over.

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:38.843
It really does.

00:03:38.843 --> 00:03:41.346
The reason to be scared is that as we're drilling through

00:03:41.346 --> 00:03:43.806
the firn, it's not staying still.

00:03:43.848 --> 00:03:46.559
As the snow and ice builds up its own weight,

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:49.979
slowly pushes it over the land toward the coasts.

00:03:49.979 --> 00:03:52.106
And then, as it reaches the end of the continent,

00:03:52.106 --> 00:03:55.109
it leaves the land and extends out over the ocean.

00:03:55.109 --> 00:03:56.861
That's called the ice shelf.

00:03:56.861 --> 00:03:59.822
The problem is, as this giant ice sheet

00:03:59.864 --> 00:04:02.242
slowly flows across the rocky ground,

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:04.619
it stretches and contracts.

00:04:04.619 --> 00:04:10.291
And if it stretches too fast, the firn snaps, creating...

00:04:11.542 --> 00:04:13.586
I'm about to go down into that crevasse.

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:15.588
You would never know it was here.

00:04:15.672 --> 00:04:17.257
Oh my God.

00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:20.677
This is a little bit of a test of my claustrophobia.

00:04:21.719 --> 00:04:22.720
Bye!

00:04:24.347 --> 00:04:26.015
Into the darkness we go.

00:04:26.557 --> 00:04:28.559
Whoa. It's huge.

00:04:31.479 --> 00:04:33.481
This is incredible.

00:04:36.484 --> 00:04:38.236
You would never think this is down here.

00:04:38.236 --> 00:04:39.612
Look how deep this is.

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:41.906
You would not want to fall down into one of these.

00:04:42.156 --> 00:04:43.908
I'm gonna...

00:04:43.908 --> 00:04:45.785
I'm gonna get out now.

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:49.539
This is the amount of time that I want to spend underground.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:51.541
So to not fall into a crevasse

00:04:51.541 --> 00:04:55.795
and to know what's coming down below us,
we need to do a lot of prep.

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:58.798
So back in the 1950s, scientists would explode

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.134
TNT on the surface to measure

00:05:01.134 --> 00:05:04.012
the seismic waves that would bounce back through the ice.

00:05:04.053 --> 00:05:07.181
Today, they shoot lasers from satellites

00:05:07.181 --> 00:05:10.893
that bounce off the surface, and any wrinkles

00:05:10.893 --> 00:05:14.022
or sagging give us hints at what's below.

00:05:14.063 --> 00:05:16.399
TNT, space lasers. Scientists are awesome.

00:05:19.152 --> 00:05:19.694
But to

00:05:19.694 --> 00:05:22.905
really get underneath in detail, they mostly shoot ice

00:05:22.905 --> 00:05:25.700
penetrating radar down from planes

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.452
like this plane!

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:31.205
They crisscross the continent,
filling in a picture of what's

00:05:31.205 --> 00:05:33.249
really underneath Antarctica.

00:05:33.249 --> 00:05:38.588
And what they can now
see is the outline of an ancient world.

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:44.469
It's not flat.

00:05:44.469 --> 00:05:47.430
There's a trench deeper than the Grand Canyon,

00:05:47.472 --> 00:05:49.932
a completely hidden mountain range

00:05:49.932 --> 00:05:52.143
that rivals the European Alps.

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:54.771
Evidence of a massive impact crater

00:05:54.771 --> 00:05:58.399
and the largest volcanic region on Earth.

00:05:58.399 --> 00:06:01.944
But in the late 60s, as they were flying these ice
penetrating

00:06:01.944 --> 00:06:06.532
radar planes,
they suddenly spotted something especially strange.

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:09.202
"They also saw these weird depressions, a really big one,:

00:06:09.202 --> 00:06:12.580
like flat spots? "Yeah, sort of a tiny depression"

00:06:12.622 --> 00:06:17.043
That earlier research with the TNT had hinted
that there was something odd here too.

00:06:17.043 --> 00:06:21.631
And that Soviet drilling team?
They're drilling right on top of it.

00:06:21.672 --> 00:06:23.257
So let's go deeper.

00:06:23.257 --> 00:06:24.258
The deeper we go.

00:06:24.258 --> 00:06:28.096
It's like we're going back in time, layers upon layers

00:06:28.137 --> 00:06:30.098
stacked on top of each other.

00:06:30.098 --> 00:06:33.351
The pores in the spongy firn get squished

00:06:33.351 --> 00:06:35.603
and the air inside gets locked in,

00:06:35.645 --> 00:06:38.856
creating little bubbles from that time in history.

00:06:38.856 --> 00:06:43.486
So now we're passing the Renaissance, the Roman Empire,

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:48.408
ancient Egypt, when humans started farming,

00:06:48.658 --> 00:06:51.577
there's the extinction of Neanderthals

00:06:51.744 --> 00:06:54.330
and the first modern humans.

00:06:54.372 --> 00:06:58.084
"And you can analyze that record by drilling a core

00:06:58.709 --> 00:07:00.461
all the way down through the ice."

00:07:00.461 --> 00:07:02.463
Wow. This is not easy.

00:07:04.507 --> 00:07:06.509
Is it going down?!

00:07:06.884 --> 00:07:09.303
I can't tell!

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:14.142
Oh, awesome.

00:07:18.146 --> 00:07:20.314
"So here you can tell the story of the ice.

00:07:20.314 --> 00:07:22.984
So if you hold it up against the light, you can see some of the layers."

00:07:23.067 --> 00:07:25.069
I see it! Wow.

00:07:27.697 --> 00:07:28.948
Look at all these bubbles.

00:07:28.948 --> 00:07:32.702
Those are pieces of atmosphere
from decades or centuries ago.

00:07:32.702 --> 00:07:34.537
But it's not just bubbles.

00:07:34.537 --> 00:07:37.582
It's also sort of these tree rings of different.

00:07:37.748 --> 00:07:38.624
I don't know what to call it.

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:40.626
Shades of ice? "Exactly."

00:07:40.751 --> 00:07:44.630
But if you hold it up, you can see that
there are actually lines in it.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:46.883
Ice that forms in winter has a different texture

00:07:46.883 --> 00:07:50.011
and composition than summer ice, creating these lines.

00:07:50.011 --> 00:07:52.472
And you can count them like tree rings,

00:07:52.472 --> 00:07:55.349
but you can also see huge sudden changes:

00:07:55.349 --> 00:07:56.350
"Where you can see

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:58.519
certain events like a volcano eruption,

00:07:58.519 --> 00:07:59.479
for instance."

00:07:59.479 --> 00:08:00.480
Like this!

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.192
That dark line is ash from a volcano eruption

00:08:04.233 --> 00:08:06.861
over 20,000 years ago.

00:08:06.861 --> 00:08:10.156
And as I was looking at these layers of exposed ice,

00:08:10.156 --> 00:08:12.700
I realized why scientists said

00:08:12.742 --> 00:08:16.746
were not allowed to do something
while we're hiking on Antarctica.

00:08:16.787 --> 00:08:19.207
This is why you can't pee on the ice.

00:08:19.248 --> 00:08:24.128
Because if you did, one day some descendant of yours

00:08:24.128 --> 00:08:27.423
would be taking an ice core in Antarctica,

00:08:27.465 --> 00:08:30.009
and they'd dig and dig and dig and they'd pull it out

00:08:30.009 --> 00:08:32.261
and they'd say, “What's that yellow line?”

00:08:32.303 --> 00:08:34.680
It's a funny way to understand what's really

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.933
so important about this ice.

00:08:36.974 --> 00:08:39.852
It's this incredible record of Earth

00:08:39.936 --> 00:08:42.522
and life on Earth that we can learn from.

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:46.901
"For me, one of the most amazing, cool science things

00:08:47.193 --> 00:08:50.780
is that a team from Europe has found a core.

00:08:51.405 --> 00:08:55.660
It's 1.3 million years and pulled they it out,

00:08:55.785 --> 00:08:58.704
and they haven't yet analyzed it, but it's in the lab."

00:08:58.746 --> 00:08:59.497
Oh, that’s so exciting.

00:08:59.497 --> 00:09:00.915
When can we expect it?

00:09:00.915 --> 00:09:03.626
"I reckon in the next couple of years." Awesome.

00:09:03.668 --> 00:09:07.129
"And that'll be the longest history

00:09:07.129 --> 00:09:09.882
of the planet's climate in ice."

00:09:11.926 --> 00:09:13.928
Oh my God.

00:09:17.306 --> 00:09:20.643
As I was hiking, I said how lifeless everything looked.

00:09:20.643 --> 00:09:23.771
And then Dr. Chown told me to go find

00:09:23.813 --> 00:09:26.607
a white rock and look underneath it.

00:09:26.649 --> 00:09:29.110
It became kind of a mission as we were hiking around,

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:31.988
and at first I really didn't understand why.

00:09:32.071 --> 00:09:33.364
Mico, I went for a hike.

00:09:33.364 --> 00:09:38.202
I'm in Antarctica right now
and I was picking up white rocks.

00:09:38.202 --> 00:09:41.205
Why would under a white rock be a good place

00:09:41.205 --> 00:09:44.250
to find life, as opposed to just under any rock?

00:09:44.292 --> 00:09:48.170
"Those white rocks can let a little bit of light
filter through,

00:09:48.212 --> 00:09:50.673
almost like a natural greenhouse effect

00:09:50.673 --> 00:09:52.675
for those tiny organisms underneath.

00:09:52.717 --> 00:09:55.386
It's a really neat adaptation." So this is Mico.

00:09:55.386 --> 00:09:58.389
It's like Clippy, but way smarter, actually.

00:09:58.431 --> 00:09:59.265
Wait, hold on one second.

00:09:59.265 --> 00:10:01.100
There's a secret way to turn it into.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:03.185
Yeah! I find that talking out

00:10:03.185 --> 00:10:06.314
loud to it sometimes helps me figure out
what I'm most interested in,

00:10:06.314 --> 00:10:10.276
and then I can go deeper into the research and figure out
which way I want to go with the story.

00:10:10.318 --> 00:10:13.529
I'm still on the hunt for a white rock with life underneath.

00:10:13.571 --> 00:10:15.531
Now, we're almost at the lakes

00:10:15.531 --> 00:10:19.452
at around 3500m or over two miles deep.

00:10:19.493 --> 00:10:22.371
The ice has completely changed.

00:10:22.371 --> 00:10:23.748
There are no bubbles.

00:10:23.748 --> 00:10:25.041
There's no dust.

00:10:25.041 --> 00:10:27.918
It's so clear you could read a book through it.

00:10:27.918 --> 00:10:31.547
This ice isn't coming from compressed fallen snow.

00:10:31.589 --> 00:10:34.300
It's coming ...from below.

00:10:34.342 --> 00:10:37.678
After decades of drilling and radar
and seismic and satellite data

00:10:37.678 --> 00:10:40.640
there was only one explanation.

00:10:40.848 --> 00:10:43.476
A lake. And not just any lake.

00:10:43.517 --> 00:10:45.519
It's huge.

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:49.857
It's similar in size to Lake Ontario.

00:10:49.857 --> 00:10:53.402
By volume, this is the sixth largest lake in the world.

00:10:53.402 --> 00:10:56.530
It's called Lake Vostok.

00:10:56.656 --> 00:11:01.202
But wait, how could there be liquid water
under all this ice?

00:11:01.243 --> 00:11:04.914
Well, for one, the ice is acting
like a giant thermal blanket.

00:11:04.955 --> 00:11:07.583
Heat from Earth's core is held in place down here.

00:11:07.583 --> 00:11:11.003
But more importantly, sitting under all this ice is

00:11:11.003 --> 00:11:15.925
~350 times more pressure than what you feel at sea level.

00:11:15.925 --> 00:11:19.303
And as pressure rises, water stays

00:11:19.303 --> 00:11:21.764
liquid at lower temperatures,

00:11:21.764 --> 00:11:26.310
which means ...a massive under ice lake is possible.

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:30.648
So now the question is, if there's water down there,
could there be life?

00:11:30.648 --> 00:11:33.234
And if it was isolated from the rest of the world

00:11:33.234 --> 00:11:35.403
for 15 million years,

00:11:35.444 --> 00:11:37.780
what would it look like?

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:40.241
With a drill poised right above it

00:11:40.241 --> 00:11:42.159
They now have to decide.

00:11:42.159 --> 00:11:43.828
Should they poke it?

00:11:43.828 --> 00:11:45.746
"They realized they could potentially

00:11:45.746 --> 00:11:48.708
go all the way through into the lake water."

00:11:48.749 --> 00:11:50.251
Was that risky?

00:11:50.251 --> 00:11:51.043
"Yeah. Oh, totally.

00:11:51.043 --> 00:11:52.294
Nobody knew what would happen.

00:11:52.294 --> 00:11:55.047
Like, would it pressurize and blow everything out?

00:11:55.089 --> 00:11:58.968
Would it, you know,
would it be really super dangerous?"

00:11:59.009 --> 00:12:01.345
"We actually looked at this very closely

00:12:01.387 --> 00:12:03.389
because we don't want to kill anybody."

00:12:03.514 --> 00:12:04.056
Oh, good.

00:12:04.056 --> 00:12:07.518
That's John Priscu,
one of the first scientists to study Lake Vostok.

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:10.730
"If you take a Pepsi bottle and you put in a Mentos,

00:12:10.771 --> 00:12:14.275
these little minty things, and blow it
in, it just blows up.

00:12:14.275 --> 00:12:15.609
So that's what we're looking at.

00:12:15.609 --> 00:12:19.113
So Lake Vostok had a lot of water
and be a blowout like a volcano.

00:12:19.155 --> 00:12:21.866
This took about ten years to sort out.

00:12:21.866 --> 00:12:23.909
It took a good portion of my life."

00:12:23.909 --> 00:12:27.705
But during this time, studies
kept finding more and more lakes.

00:12:27.747 --> 00:12:28.831
Hundreds of them.

00:12:28.831 --> 00:12:32.626
And satellites revealed something very weird.

00:12:32.668 --> 00:12:36.213
The ice sheet was lifting and dropping

00:12:36.255 --> 00:12:39.425
almost ten meters in some places,

00:12:39.425 --> 00:12:41.802
and Dr. Helen Fricker was the first to notice.

00:12:41.844 --> 00:12:42.970
"Yeah, it was pretty incredible.

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:45.139
It was one of those, like, jaw-dropping moments."

00:12:45.181 --> 00:12:48.976
While Vostok and other lakes like it
sat sealed off around the continent,

00:12:48.976 --> 00:12:51.145
there were hundreds of other active lakes

00:12:51.145 --> 00:12:55.107
that can literally lift and drop parts of the ice sheet

00:12:55.149 --> 00:12:58.027
as they filled and drained into one another.

00:12:58.027 --> 00:13:00.863
And the first one that she saw was here.

00:13:01.113 --> 00:13:03.574
"The whole lake shape looks like a footprint."

00:13:03.574 --> 00:13:05.659
So there aren't just lakes down there.

00:13:05.659 --> 00:13:08.162
It's an entire plumbing system

00:13:08.204 --> 00:13:11.040
under the ice connected by rivers.

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:13.250
So at this point, over a decade had passed since

00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:16.587
drilling had paused at Vostok and we'd learned so much.

00:13:16.587 --> 00:13:20.716
So now the plan wasn't for one country
to drill into one lake.

00:13:20.758 --> 00:13:24.220
It was for three countries to drill into three

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:27.556
lakes: the Russians into Lake Vostok, the British

00:13:27.556 --> 00:13:31.143
into Lake Ellsworth, and the Americans into Lake Whillans.

00:13:31.185 --> 00:13:33.145
Time to poke an ancient lake!

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:34.522
First up, the Russians.

00:13:34.522 --> 00:13:37.942
They had a massive head start with their effort
to drill into Lake Vostok.

00:13:37.942 --> 00:13:39.235
But they had a problem.

00:13:39.235 --> 00:13:41.487
There hole was already filled with drill fluid

00:13:41.529 --> 00:13:44.490
to prevent it from freezing, specifically kerosene.

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:47.493
So to solve this problem,
they added freon, which is heavier,

00:13:47.493 --> 00:13:49.703
and the idea was that it would act like a plug.

00:13:49.703 --> 00:13:53.123
And then they used the special drill with a heated tip

00:13:53.123 --> 00:13:55.751
to slowly melt into the surface of the lake.

00:13:55.751 --> 00:13:58.921
And as soon as they broke through,
the pressurized water from the lake,

00:13:58.963 --> 00:14:01.465
shot up over 30m into the hole,

00:14:01.465 --> 00:14:03.801
pushing the kerosene and the freon away.

00:14:03.843 --> 00:14:07.388
"I saw the videos and there was kerosene
blowing at the top of that hole.

00:14:07.429 --> 00:14:09.932
It was a little scary. I'd be running as fast as I could."

00:14:09.974 --> 00:14:12.142
They left that new lake water in the hole

00:14:12.184 --> 00:14:15.312
to freeze for a season,
and then they pulled it up to study it,

00:14:15.312 --> 00:14:17.356
and the headlines said that they found

00:14:17.356 --> 00:14:21.986
a totally new type of bacteria! But those bacteria

00:14:22.027 --> 00:14:25.573
were suspiciously similar to the bacteria found in kerosene.

00:14:25.614 --> 00:14:29.535
"Problem was that it mixed with the drilling fluid,
which is highly contaminated.

00:14:29.535 --> 00:14:30.786
It's full of bacteria.

00:14:30.786 --> 00:14:33.289
so they didn't publish anything."

00:14:33.289 --> 00:14:34.123
"That was controversial

00:14:34.123 --> 00:14:36.333
because many people said

00:14:36.333 --> 00:14:38.460
they had contaminated with drill fluid."

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:41.046
So the international science community was skeptical

00:14:41.046 --> 00:14:44.216
of the Russian find, but the British and the American teams

00:14:44.216 --> 00:14:48.345
were still trying to poke their lakes,
and they were using a different drilling tech

00:14:48.387 --> 00:14:52.141
that didn't use drill fluid, but super clean hot water

00:14:52.182 --> 00:14:55.102
that was filtered and decontaminated with UV light.

00:14:55.144 --> 00:14:59.064
This boiling, pressurized water
just blasts and melts away the ice,

00:14:59.064 --> 00:15:02.026
but it still needed fuel to power the thing.

00:15:02.026 --> 00:15:05.112
And the British team, they ran out.

00:15:05.446 --> 00:15:07.489
The other teams tried to help them

00:15:07.489 --> 00:15:09.783
get more fuel, but they couldn't.

00:15:09.825 --> 00:15:11.994
So now, with only one team left,

00:15:12.036 --> 00:15:15.039
they slowly went deeper and deeper

00:15:15.039 --> 00:15:18.417
and deeper until finally...

00:15:20.377 --> 00:15:21.503
"And I remember breaking

00:15:21.503 --> 00:15:24.590
into the lake and you could hear the cheers.
The whole roar went up.

00:15:24.590 --> 00:15:25.299
It was pretty...

00:15:25.299 --> 00:15:27.801
I had goosebumps and we broke through."

00:15:27.801 --> 00:15:31.347
So they bring up a sample of liquid water from this lake,

00:15:31.347 --> 00:15:35.351
and inside they find life.

00:15:35.809 --> 00:15:39.355
Tons of life! A full ecosystem

00:15:39.355 --> 00:15:42.066
with colonies of bacteria and tiny organisms

00:15:42.066 --> 00:15:45.444
that were all thriving without sunlight.

00:15:45.444 --> 00:15:48.197
And in 2018, they did it again

00:15:48.238 --> 00:15:50.324
here, this time sampling sediment too.

00:15:50.324 --> 00:15:52.868
Dr. Priscu gave us a sneak peek of the research

00:15:52.868 --> 00:15:54.954
that he's about to publish from that lake.

00:15:54.954 --> 00:15:59.625
And it seems to show that some of the life under Antarctica

00:15:59.625 --> 00:16:04.964
is genuinely new and different than anywhere else on Earth.

00:16:04.964 --> 00:16:06.966
"We found some new kinds of viruses

00:16:06.966 --> 00:16:09.301
that are bacteria viruses."

00:16:09.343 --> 00:16:11.929
Don't worry.
These are viruses that are specific to bacteria.

00:16:11.929 --> 00:16:14.306
They're not going to infect you.
I was worried about that too.

00:16:14.306 --> 00:16:15.599
I want to study them.

00:16:15.599 --> 00:16:17.393
I just want to never get them.

00:16:17.393 --> 00:16:19.186
"No, I don't think you’re gonna get ‘em.

00:16:19.186 --> 00:16:21.146
I mean, I'm still alive."

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:22.439
Do you think.

00:16:22.439 --> 00:16:25.442
Do you think that there are larger

00:16:25.442 --> 00:16:28.070
animals down there that we haven't seen yet?

00:16:28.529 --> 00:16:30.197
"Yeah. That's another. That's a good question.

00:16:30.197 --> 00:16:33.534
We looked around, we took an ROV and we had cameras going,

00:16:33.575 --> 00:16:36.954
so unless they were avoiding us, there’s not."

00:16:36.954 --> 00:16:40.457
But that new microbial life is really special

00:16:40.499 --> 00:16:42.543
because while it's new to us...

00:16:42.584 --> 00:16:45.546
"So Antarctica hasn't always been fully covered with ice

00:16:45.838 --> 00:16:49.508
So maybe the new life we find is old life. Old new life."

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:51.510
Using this new research, we can learn

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:57.224
about the origins of life on our planet and even beyond it.

00:16:57.266 --> 00:16:59.226
One of the coolest things about this

00:16:59.226 --> 00:17:01.395
story is that all of this science,

00:17:01.437 --> 00:17:03.397
all of this drilling through incredibly

00:17:03.397 --> 00:17:06.817
thick ice and not contaminating it or us, and all of this

00:17:06.817 --> 00:17:10.946
technology is exactly what we would use to explore

00:17:10.946 --> 00:17:15.325
the icy moons and planets for alien life.

00:17:15.576 --> 00:17:19.038
Finding life here makes many scientists

00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:21.749
think that we'll find it out there.

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:24.668
Do you think that there is life on the icy

00:17:24.668 --> 00:17:27.087
moons like Europa?

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:29.131
"Yes. Yes, definitely."

00:17:29.131 --> 00:17:32.760
We found life in one of the coldest, darkest,

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:35.220
most alien places on Earth.

00:17:35.262 --> 00:17:39.224
There are hundreds of other lakes still untouched.

00:17:39.266 --> 00:17:43.103
And while scientists think it's very unlikely
that any of them contain

00:17:43.103 --> 00:17:46.732
larger life forms, what's inside is still a mystery.

00:17:46.774 --> 00:17:47.357
"It's just cool.

00:17:47.357 --> 00:17:50.694
Science is amazing and humans are amazing for doing this."

00:17:50.736 --> 00:17:53.238
"We have a lot more to explore."

00:17:53.405 --> 00:17:55.866
Exploring has never been easy.

00:17:55.866 --> 00:17:58.660
"The teamwork involves so many people involved.

00:17:58.827 --> 00:18:01.205
You know, it's just an incredible thing."

00:18:01.497 --> 00:18:02.748
but it's worth it.

00:18:02.998 --> 00:18:05.626
There are still untouched places

00:18:05.626 --> 00:18:09.630
left to be discovered and new mysteries to solve.

00:18:10.589 --> 00:18:11.173
HUGE*, if true.

00:18:11.173 --> 00:18:14.927
We're making another episode
all about what really happened

00:18:14.927 --> 00:18:17.012
with the ozone layer on Antarctica

00:18:17.012 --> 00:18:19.306
and the current science happening right now.

00:18:19.306 --> 00:18:21.308
So if you want to know more, subscribe.

